<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chronicle of Albelda</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 881–883</span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> · Also known as Albeldensian Chronicle</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Chronicle of Albelda is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> chronicle compiled at the court of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-iii-of-asturias">Alfonso III of Asturias</a> around 881–883, named after the monastery of Albelda in La Rioja, where a copy was made. It combines a description of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> with lists of Roman, Visigothic and Asturian kings and a short history of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asturias">Asturias</a>. With the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chronicle-of-alfonso-iii">Chronicle of Alfonso III</a> it presents the Asturian kings as heirs of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/visigoths">Visigoths</a> destined to restore Christian rule over Spain.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chronicle-of-albelda.jpg" alt="Chronicle of Albelda" width="320" height="176" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Pertenece al Codex Vigilanus (folio 428) su probable autor sea el escriba y monje Vigila o Vigilán. (by Rockger21) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Scribes_Codex_Vigilanus.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
